<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>engram</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Engram.Cli</RootNamespace>
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
    <StripSymbols>true</StripSymbols>
    <StaticWebAssetsEnabled>false</StaticWebAssetsEnabled>

    <!--
      Three warnings, all from LLamaSharp, none reachable from Engram.

      IL2026/IL3050 come from the JSON converters attached to ModelParams: reflection-based
      Serialize/Deserialize, rooted by their [JsonConverter] attributes and never called, because
      Engram builds a ModelParams and reads it — it never round-trips one through JSON. IL3000 is
      that library's own native-path probe reading Assembly.Location, which is empty under AOT. It
      is suppressed rather than worked around: measured on the published binary, the load succeeds
      with no path configuration at all, because the probe has a working fallback behind it. See
      LlamaNative for what stating the paths instead cost when it was tried.

      Implementing IModelParams and IContextParams ourselves would avoid all three and was rejected:
      it means restating 38 llama.cpp defaults as our own constants, which trades three warnings
      about dead code for 38 chances to silently pick a different number than the engine would.

      NoWarn is per-project and cannot name an assembly, so this necessarily also covers Engram's
      own code — and that is the rule the AOT publish used to enforce for free. NoReflectionJson
      Tests replaces it: every JsonSerializer call in src/ must pass a source-generated JsonTypeInfo.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL3050;IL3000</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Engram.Core/Engram.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ModelContextProtocol" />
    <PackageReference Include="ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Engram.Integration.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
